{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2229 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "20bad9ce4a594d819409ea0e79955d536d0f55604fb6b5c5354dcb34bac1a163" :edb/db-before-t 2228 :edb/db-after-t 2229 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535541 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:08:58.309Z" 13194139535541 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354356 13194139535541 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354359 13194139535541 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354363 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354357 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1089 "q-351003" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{ft}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12{t}^{3}+20t,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=25\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1113 #uuid "ef5a528e-3d29-5ffb-a668-9fd133c57543" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354356 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (12{t}^{3}+20t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=25.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =3({1}^{4})+10({1}^{2})+C \\\\ 25 & =13+C \\\\ C & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+12.$" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354358 13194139535541 true] [17592186354357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535541 true] [17592186354357 1041 "field-1" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354358 13194139535541 true] [17592186354357 1110 #uuid "d42913a8-486c-5cdb-b6ff-a9765aa155fd" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354358 1034 "3t^4+10t^2+12" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535541 true] [17592186354358 1111 #uuid "f0b128cb-762b-5ac3-9e2e-ab6bd5e30399" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354360 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1089 "q-351027" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m /s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m /s}$ when $t=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1113 #uuid "965499a4-cbc7-5edc-bbf6-55706f2f19ea" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354359 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\pi )=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+C \\\\ 10 & =-6(-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4.$" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354361 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354362 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1041 "field-1" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354362 13194139535541 true] [17592186354360 1110 #uuid "1f020414-ee06-5bdc-a0d2-e4713f6d9b12" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354361 1034 "-6\\cos(t)+10" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535541 true] [17592186354361 1111 #uuid "5f611c80-3aed-5021-b1b6-999a77942a65" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354362 1034 "-6\\cos(t)+4" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535541 true] [17592186354362 1111 #uuid "6e2e28b2-dd67-5539-a65d-4d62b7df06e5" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354364 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1089 "q-351051" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1113 #uuid "8b1822ff-b3b6-5a2a-882b-65b7f52390da" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354363 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+\\int (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{6}{2}-3(2)+C \\\\ 1 & =-9+C \\\\ C & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+10.$" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354365 13194139535541 true] [17592186354364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535541 true] [17592186354364 1041 "field-1" 13194139535541 true] [17592186354364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186